  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Gneuss is a family-owned mechanical engineering company and technology leader in plastics processing — a market position that was nowhere near as visible from outside as the company's actual technical importance would have justified.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The previous logo carried a symbol: the rotary filter disc, the company's invention that filters polymer melts continuously without interrupting production. This invention shaped the company's history — but by the time of the repositioning, Gneuss's product portfolio had long since grown far beyond it.</p>
</section>

<section class="container" style="padding-block:1rem 4rem;max-width:900px;">
  <h2 class="display-md reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Change follows strategy — down to the detail.</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">For Cobiax and DSC Arminia Bielefeld, the strategic answer was a complete logo relaunch. For Gneuss, the same analysis gave a more precise picture: it was not the whole logo that was the problem, but a single element.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The symbol visibly tied the brand to a single invention that was no longer representative — and so stood in the way of its actual direction as a broadly based technology leader. The consequence was surgical, not radical: the symbol went, the word mark stayed unchanged. Precisely this difference between a full relaunch and a targeted correction is the core of “change follows strategy” — only what the strategy actually demands is changed, no more and no less.</p>
</section>

